            </svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Lock Change vs. Lock Rekeying — Which Do You Need?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              This is one of the most common questions we get, and the honest answer is: it depends on your situation. Rekeying is faster and less expensive — it changes the internal pin configuration of your existing lock so all previous keys stop working, while keeping the same hardware in place. It's the right choice when your locks are in good condition and you simply need to void old keys.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              A full lock change makes more sense when: your existing hardware is damaged, worn out, or of poor quality; you want to upgrade to a higher security grade; you want a fundamentally different type of lock (like switching from a knob to a deadbolt, or adding a keypad); or when you want the complete assurance that every component is brand new. Lock changes cost more than rekeying, but they deliver a fully reset security baseline.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Not sure which you need? Call us at (302) 735-0050 and describe your situation. We'll give you an honest recommendation without pushing you toward the more expensive option unless it's genuinely the right call for your circumstances.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
